⁡\\theta -3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/61ffc8879681a1d0bb381f32178ea2e7b58e251341b2280ba607448231458dc5.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(0,5)$ with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $8,$ as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,5).$\n\n$x=$ \n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$ \n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(0,5),$ $a=3$ and $b=8.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327171" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327171" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327171" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/8a04ad6bb8ce108e4f63fddcd5c178ec855a646a14761ad989a9e6c9328ddbd0.png)\n\nThe semi-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the elipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6db43ff41344f292c8cb5805febd175088fe068fe89927bade0c6fc2246c8781.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [\\pi ,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106400" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{6} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{6}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{36} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "cd3c3ab57d3f4eee40fa133cc881384f001d3a25c4d860095ddb8674be6cfd87" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{36}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{6}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-15843" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{36}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/state.json" "file_sha256" "f7e17fbd21a60b56f03ed40cebf7ae0d2f64b16f2af961e0289f78fb903d56fc" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at the origin with horizontal radius $3$ and vertical radius $1,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,0)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/5d791b30556f41973c8f1a9b8702744afae72458d0ce285de2b7ef8fa19d720a.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "c051186d7c8fff3106f511a71105516c018c45a15663b739b60b2d61eeeb1006" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6814" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, $a=3$ and $b=1.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 3\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6814.json" "file_sha256" "c051186d7c8fff3106f511a71105516c018c45a15663b739b60b2d61eeeb1006" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the ellipse centered at $(1,-2)$ with horizontal radius $6$ and vertical radius $4,$ as shown below. Which set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(7,-2)?$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/4724f0bcc1499be95dc8a28584511ecdb86d078b8205ad81a6e836ba1bdd261c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "9b315a82feab1886a14fe30608a7c61e76937596abfcbbca7cd0b54946ad9aca" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6816" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta+h,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta+k, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(1,-2),$ $a=6$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta+1,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta-2,\\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6816.json" "file_sha256" "9b315a82feab1886a14fe30608a7c61e76937596abfcbbca7cd0b54946ad9aca" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The semi-ellipse below is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 4\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/6ca367eb7dc7cbd8205cbd85ad6d2503f957defd3f9a10e7b510759f41e0c778.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "ad968429e0e578f1412935b842cb4ebbe7c9dd2bc2e5e7fdfd70aba9eff81b3c" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6818" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx= a\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=b\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=5$ and $b=4.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/assets/a64898381b2cf50c71a79a3dbcf02b9a83f320b8c2d1ba542b3beecc05e17625.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0, \\pi\\right],$ we get the semi-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx= 5\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=4\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in\\left[0, \\pi\\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6818.json" "file_sha256" "ad968429e0e578f1412935b842cb4ebbe7c9dd2bc2e5e7fdfd70aba9eff81b3c" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx= 4\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta - 1, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta<2\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+1 & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{2} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using $\\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{16}+\\frac{(y+1)^2}{4} = 1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/example-6815.json" "file_sha256" "81c656737095311c331acafe47fb3ed4ebe4167da8c2eb001f4e8ed6786cc297" "basis" 1107 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004891/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/assets/14d1fc5a92c28cc7f84c1ec23929701f1aca67b1a15d6113fa83f1428354fc11.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(3,-1)$ with horizontal radius $2$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(3,-1),$ $a=2$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -1,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bd2e5e871b6c74e0b963a0c4153f5795d89d5cab6bb61fa8d50bed50b80946ba" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-78680" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/state.json" "file_sha256" "9b9dd6614890663c50e9b3ef36c2f6a7c3e29b557686b711f27370ea081c787e" "basis" 1220 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14032117/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The Cartesian equation of the ellipse defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n{{field-1}}\n$=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c50ccf2eb6eb588c96a6d1d04405f10aed49d9823dabb788d7410487869bfc6" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4\\sqrt{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{x}{4\\sqrt{6}} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{(4\\sqrt{6})^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{96} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =5\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{y}{5\\sqrt{2}} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{(5\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{50} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\n$$\n\nSo, the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{96}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{50}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c50ccf2eb6eb588c96a6d1d04405f10aed49d9823dabb788d7410487869bfc6" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c50ccf2eb6eb588c96a6d1d04405f10aed49d9823dabb788d7410487869bfc6" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-327808" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{x^2}{96}+\\frac{y^2}{50}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c50ccf2eb6eb588c96a6d1d04405f10aed49d9823dabb788d7410487869bfc6" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c50ccf2eb6eb588c96a6d1d04405f10aed49d9823dabb788d7410487869bfc6" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the ellipse, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{x}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 3y & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 9{y}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c50ccf2eb6eb588c96a6d1d04405f10aed49d9823dabb788d7410487869bfc6" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4be40ac811c9a7849b7e9caf70e564b541ed013e8fe8332c47f598abf0a761c5" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/state.json" "file_sha256" "6c50ccf2eb6eb588c96a6d1d04405f10aed49d9823dabb788d7410487869bfc6" "basis" 1471 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040724/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106406" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=1"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/ma