=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5"] ["math" "x=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-79290" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-106398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/7b9f67594871385ccf7ac02c3af9e47a585116c162a08cdc1795d452a09912fe.png)\n\nConsider the ellipse centered at $(-2,-3)$ with horizontal radius $5$ and vertical radius $3,$ as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the ellipse once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(3,-3)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-106398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at the point $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the ellipse is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(h+a,k).$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-2,-3),$ $a=5$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-106398" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f33df654564f4f131a84890341dea87fc6ce195c7505a09c3b16d77077ff5dc"} {"question" "q-106398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-106398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3"] ["math" "x=25\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=9\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2"] ["math" "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3"] ["math" "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2"] ["math" "x=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-106398" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-328327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/5234672d8176a0a04bbc3feecfe13092ff116a25682ae055286f1fe858b8a754.png)\n\nThe half-ellipse above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$ Assume $\\theta $ is between $0$ and $2\\pi .$\n\n$\\theta \\in [${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$]$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-328327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of an ellipse centered at $(h,k)$ with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ are\n\n$$\nx=a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +h,\\,y=b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +k,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the ellipse is centered at the point $(-1,2),$ $a=5,$ and $b=3.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the *full* ellipse are\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a half-ellipse, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the ellipse and the horizontal line through its center.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/assets/1b2d7617af37197dfd2702b4497263e563458b0bf11c622308220def93b688a9.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}],$ we get the half-ellipse.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the half-ellipse are \n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,\\,\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-328327" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0f33df654564f4f131a84890341dea87fc6ce195c7505a09c3b16d77077ff5dc"} {"question" "q-328327" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-328327" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a40beb2fb2100f20b741d529b498ac8924b48c"} {"question" "q-328327" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040842/state.json" "file_sha256" "4a55e2ac44e5055a40b962db83a